## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-boundary bit manipulation and 128-bit arithmetic, which are fundamental skills in cryptography and high-performance kernel development. It also evaluates the ability to implement rotation operations without branching logic.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across two 64-bit limbs
- Using a single 128-bit intermediate simplifies boundary handling
- Edge-case handling via rotation count masking (eliminates branches)

## Code Example

```cpp
void rotl128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  __uint128_t v = ((__uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;
  k &= 127;
  __uint128_t r = (v << k) | (v >> (128 - k));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)r;
}
```

## Edge Cases

- `k = 0`: Returns original value unchanged
- `k = 64`: Swaps hi and lo parts completely

## Why This Is Asked
This tests advanced bit manipulation skills, requiring multi-word arithmetic without 128-bit native support. It demonstrates branchless programming techniques and careful handling of shift operations.

## Key Concepts
- Represent 128-bit values as two 64-bit words (hi, lo)
- Decompose k into t = k mod 64 (intra-word shift) and b = k div 64 (cross-word flag)
- Branchless selection using bitwise operations and masks
- Handle edge cases like k=0 and k=64 without conditionals

## Code Example
```c
void rotate_left_128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned k, 
                    uint64_t* out_hi, uint64_t* out_lo) {
    uint64_t t = k & 63;
    uint64_t b = (k >> 6) & 1;

    uint64_t hiA = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loA = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t hiB = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loB = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));

    *out_hi = hiA ^ ((hiA ^ hiB) & -b);
    *out_lo = loA ^ ((loA ^ loB) & -b);
}
```

## Examples
- k=1, hi=0, lo=1 → out_hi=1, out_lo=0
- k=64, hi=1, lo=0 → out_hi=0, out_lo=1

