## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a branchless popcount for 128-bit data using only bitwise ops, a core skill in performance-critical code paths like fingerprinting or anomaly detection.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless bit counting (SWAR)
- Apply per-64-bit halves independently
- Combine results without overflow or branches

## Code Example

```javascript
// Conceptual: 64-bit helper (C-like pseudocode)
unsigned int popcount64(unsigned long long x){
  x -= (x>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if you needed to accumulate counts in a streaming fashion?
- How does this interact with endianness in cross-language bindings?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) bit counting and handling of 128-bit data without branching.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR-based popcount
- 128-bit data split into 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling tricks

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned pop64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (unsigned)(v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

