## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep knowledge of bit manipulation techniques for combinatorial enumeration without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Next higher number with same popcount (snoob algorithm)
- Handling 64-bit overflow with masking
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts only
- Edge cases are constant-time checks

## Code Example
```javascript
function nextPermutation64(v) {
  const MASK = (1n << 64n) - 1n;
  if (v === 0n || v === MASK) return 0n;
  const c = v & -v;
  const ripple = v + c;
  const ones = v ^ ripple;
  const next = ripple | ((ones >> 2n) / c);
  return next & MASK;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to perform a branchless, cross-boundary bitwise scan on a 128-bit value—a common primitive in cryptography and systems programming. It verifies you can correctly propagate carries and parity across the 64-bit lane boundary without branching.

## Key Concepts

- **Parallel prefix (scan) technique**: Each shift-XOR step doubles the bit-range being folded, achieving O(log n) instead of O(n) operations.
- **128-bit right shift implementation**: Requires explicit carry propagation between the hi and lo 64-bit words.
- **Branchless design**: No conditionals ensures constant-time execution, critical for crypto applications.

## Code Example

```javascript
function prefixXor128(x_lo, x_hi) {
  let lo = x_lo, hi = x_hi;
  // 128-bit shift helper: (lo, hi) >> k
  const shift128 = (lo, hi, k) => {
    if (k < 64) return { lo: (lo >>> k) | ((hi & ((1n << k) - 1n)) << (64 - k)), hi: hi >>> k };
    return { lo: hi >>> (k - 64), hi: 0 };
  };
  // Parallel prefix: x ^= x >> {1,2,4,8,16,32,64}
  for (const k of [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64]) {
    const s = shift128(lo, hi, k);
    lo ^= s.lo; hi ^= s.hi;
  }
  return { lo, hi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a prefix OR or prefix AND?
- What changes are needed to handle SIMD/vector implementations?
- How does this compare to a naive loop-based approach in terms of cycle count?

## Why This Is Asked
Branchless 128-bit comparison is common in crypto/hash pipelines and requires careful borrow logic across halves. It tests low-level reasoning without relying on 128-bit native ops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit numbers split into hi/lo.
- Borrow propagation across 64-bit halves.
- Branchless evaluation using comparisons on unsigned integers.

## Code Example
```c
uint8_t lessThan128(uint128 a, uint128 b){
  uint64_t loDiff = a.lo - b.lo;
  uint64_t borrowLo = (a.lo < b.lo);
  uint64_t hiDiff = a.hi - b.hi - borrowLo;
  uint64_t borrowHi = (a.hi < b.hi + borrowLo);
  return (uint8_t)borrowHi;
}
```

